---
import Docs from "../layouts/Docs.astro";
import { getDocsSections } from "../lib/docs";

const sections = await getDocsSections();
---

<Docs
  description="Learn how to run and control Lowland Linux machines in browsers and Node.js."
  current=""
  headings={[]}
>
  <h1>Lowland documentation</h1>
  <p>
    Lowland is the Linux kernel ported to WebAssembly. A machine boots inside a browser tab or a
    Node.js process, runs Linux programs compiled for WebAssembly, and gives your JavaScript a
    handle to start those programs, move files in and out, and decide what the network can reach.
  </p>
  <p>
    These pages assume you are comfortable with a Linux shell and with modern JavaScript. They
    spend their words on what is different here, not on how Linux or streams work. The guide
    reads in order, and over its chapters it builds a small service that runs code snippets
    sent by users. Each chapter also makes sense on its own. If you want to know how Lowland
    compares to v86 or WebContainers first, read the <a href="/faq/">questions</a>.
  </p>

  {sections.map(({ heading, items }) => (
    <>
      <h2>{heading}</h2>
      <ul class="contents">
        {items.map((item) => (
          <li>
            <a href={item.href}>{item.code ? <code>{item.label}</code> : item.label}</a>
            <p>{item.description}</p>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </>
  ))}
</Docs>

<style>
  .contents {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;

    li + li {
      margin-block-start: 0.75rem;
    }

    a {
      color: var(--code-foreground);
    }

    p {
      margin: 0;
    }
  }
</style>
